# `sysctl.set` observes the file it writes as well as the running kernel (#658).
#
# Right-shaped but wrong (adverse-cases.md): the live kernel already holds the value, and the
# drop-in under /etc/sysctl.d is **gone** — a host where somebody ran `sysctl -w` by hand, or
# where the file was cleaned up. The observe read the kernel alone, said `already`, and the
# setting was lost at the next reboot, which is the one thing this def exists to prevent.
#
# The mirror of `adverse-sysctl.set.shellf`, which breaks the kernel and leaves the file: there
# the apply runs, so its assertion on the file passes without testing the observe.
#
# A `net.` key, never a `vm.` one: sysctl keys outside the network namespace are shared with the
# host, so a privileged container writing `vm.swappiness` changes the developer's own machine.
# A different key from the other case, so the two cannot interact whatever order they run in.
on target {
    as root {
        unsafe shell {
            rm -f /etc/sysctl.d/90-shellf-net-ipv4-tcp_fin_timeout.conf
            sysctl -w net.ipv4.tcp_fin_timeout=45 >/dev/null
            # The state really is the converged-looking one, or the case would pass for the
            # wrong reason.
            [ "$(sysctl -n net.ipv4.tcp_fin_timeout)" = "45" ] || exit 1
            [ ! -f /etc/sysctl.d/90-shellf-net-ipv4-tcp_fin_timeout.conf ] || exit 1
        }
        sysctl.set("net.ipv4.tcp_fin_timeout", "45")
        shell {
            grep -qxF 'net.ipv4.tcp_fin_timeout=45' \
                /etc/sysctl.d/90-shellf-net-ipv4-tcp_fin_timeout.conf || exit 1
            [ "$(sysctl -n net.ipv4.tcp_fin_timeout)" = "45" ] || exit 1
        }
    }
}
